course: dictionary
lesson: 13596
title: Proof you cannot argue with
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Incontrovertibly is a long word, but its parts give clues. In- means not, controvert means argue against, and -ly makes it an adverb. So the word means in a way nobody can argue against. Ask yourself: are the facts so clear that nobody could disagree? If yes, this word fits.
explain: «The footprints in the mud incontrovertibly showed that the thief had run towards the river.» The footprints are clear evidence, and the word tells the reader the question is closed. It is stronger than probably or maybe.
explain: You will meet this word in serious writing: science reports, detective stories and maths. In maths, a proof shows something incontrovertibly. A good check is to swap in certainly or without any doubt. If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Incontrovertibly is an adverb, from the adjective incontrovertible.
fact: Incontrovertibly means in a way nobody can argue against or prove wrong.
fact: It also means without any possible doubt; certainly.
fact: The parts help: in- means not, and controvert means to argue against.
fact: It is a formal word, so you will see it more often in writing than in everyday talk.
exercise: «The test results incontrovertibly proved that the medicine worked.» What does incontrovertibly mean here? | Only just | *In a way nobody can argue against | Very slowly | With a loud noise
exercise: Incontrovertibly comes from which adjective? | controversial | inconvenient | *incontrovertible
exercise: Ava says maybe means the same as incontrovertibly. What is the difference? | Incontrovertibly means it is a question | Maybe means there is no doubt at all, and incontrovertibly means it might not be true | *Incontrovertibly means there is no doubt at all, while maybe means it might not be true | They mean exactly the same, so there is no difference
test: «The camera recording incontrovertibly showed that the ball had crossed the line.» What does incontrovertibly show? | The camera was broken | *The evidence is so clear that nobody can argue | The ball may have crossed the line | The players were angry
test: Which sentence uses the word correctly? | *The photos incontrovertibly proved that the vase was broken by the cat | I think it might rain, so I incontrovertibly brought an umbrella | She incontrovertibly might be tired | The sky is incontrovertibly a sandwich
exam: Which word best replaces incontrovertibly in «The results incontrovertibly proved the theory»? | Slowly | *Certainly | Possibly | Loudly
